{"id":233,"created_when":"2026-11-10T08:57:49Z","created_by":"arun","updated_when":"2026-11-10T08:57:49Z","updated_by":"arun","advisory_id":"SNWLID-2026-0021","title":"Informational Advisory: SonicOS Client DPI-SSL Trust of Compromised eMudhra Certificate Chain","published_when":"2026-11-10T08:57:49Z","last_updated_when":"2026-11-10T08:57:49Z","impact":"not_defined","cvss":null,"cvss_vector":null,"cvss_version":3.0,"cwe":null,"cve":null,"cpe":"","summary":"<p>SonicWall has addressed an issue in SonicOS where appliances with <b>Client DPI-SSL</b> enabled continued to trust certificate chains issued by compromised <b>eMudhra intermediate Certificate Authorities (CAs)</b>. Because the firewall’s local certificate store trusted the underlying root certificates, the firewall re-signed traffic from impacted servers with the local SonicWall DPI-SSL CA certificate, preventing client browsers from rejecting connections signed by the untrusted intermediate CAs.</p><p>\r\nSonicWall has remediated this issue by removing the affected root certificates from the SonicOS certificate store and implementing blocking logic for the compromised intermediate certificate chain. SonicWall strongly recommends that customers upgrade to the latest release versions listed below to address and mitigate the associated risks.</p>","is_workaround_available":false,"workaround":"None.","detail":"","affected_products":"<table class=\"MsoNormalTable\" border=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" width=\"629\" style=\"color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: &quot;Nunito Sans&quot;, sans-serif; font-size: 13px; background-image: initial; background-position: initial; background-size: initial; background-repeat: initial; background-attachment: initial; background-origin: initial; background-clip: initial; width: 471.5pt;\"><tbody><tr style=\"height: 0.2in;\"><td width=\"413\" nowrap=\"nowrap\" valign=\"bottom\" style=\"width: 310pt; border: 1pt solid windowtext; background: rgb(237, 125, 49); padding: 0